Offset: faces whose offset vanishes within the offset distance
(mitcad#172). Offset inside, a face narrower than what the offsets of its
neighbours take from it (a chamfer strip between faces meeting at an
angle) or a convex rounding of a radius smaller than the offset turns
inside out: the images of its edges cross. BRepOffset_MakeOffset kept
such a face and failed (intersection join: the thick solid had fewer
faces than the body; a closed offset no solid) or built a wrong solid (the
inverted rounding kept; with the arc join, the thick solid came back as
the body itself). Outside, the same holds for concave strips.

The faces of four edges whose offset turns inside out between two
opposite edges all along them are found before the offsets are built
(FindVanishingFaces): at stations along one edge, the images of a point
and of the nearest point of the opposite edge on the boundary of the
face's offset (moved along the normal at smooth or free edges, where the
offsets of the two faces meet at sharp ones) come in the opposite order.
Such a face gets no offset. The faces beyond the two edges, past further
vanishing faces in a row, have their offsets intersected with each other
instead (BRepOffset_Inter3d::ConnexIntAcrossVanished for the intersection
join, ConnexIntByArcAcrossVanished for the arc join); the intersection
stands for the image of their edges to the vanishing faces. The edges to
vanishing faces are left out of the walks round the wires of the faces
(BRepOffset_Inter2d::ConnexIntByInt), so that the edges on both sides of
them meet, and the smooth edges there are cut within their offsets
instead of being extended. A face stays where it vanishes only in parts,
where a free or open edge lies beyond it, and with the arc join where a
tube runs along one of its edges. The complete intersection of planar
shapes, which removes invalid faces its own way, and thickening are
unchanged.

A thick solid to which the offset contributes no face (the faces of the
body alone, the offset having failed) is no longer done.

